Your answer lacks clarity on how to manage edge cases, such as empty strings or strings with no common prefix. To improve, you could implement a loop that iterates over the characters of the shortest string in the array (since the common prefix cannot be longer than the shortest string) and checks if all strings share the same character at each position. If a mismatch is found, you return the substring from the start up to that point.

For example, you might write something like this:

```java
public String longestCommonPrefix(String[] strs) {
    if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) return "";
    String prefix = strs[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < strs.length; i++) {
        while (strs[i].indexOf(prefix) != 0) {
            prefix = prefix.substring(0, prefix.length() - 1);
            if (prefix.isEmpty()) return "";
        }
    }
    return prefix;
}
```

This approach ensures that you correctly identify the longest common prefix efficiently. Keep practicing with different inputs to solidify your understanding and improve your implementation. Good job on starting to think about edge cases!

